INT. FIELD KITCHEN IN A RUINED OPERA HOUSE - LATE AFTERNOON

Plaster cherubs on the ceiling, dust on the velvet seats. In the orchestra pit, a canvas-covered stove ticks and hisses. A gaping hole in the roof lets a shaft of watery sunlight fall onto the stage, where a dozen mess tins steam in a row.

CAPTAIN ANNELIES DE VRIES, 40s, reading glasses pushed up into gray-blond hair, leans on a conductor's stand with a ledger. SERGEANT IGNACY WRÓBEL, 50s, thick mustache, stirs a vat of barley soup with an oar-length spoon. CORPORAL EZRA PRYCE, 20s, peels potatoes on the lip of the stage with excruciating care. PRIVATE LUCIEN BEAUMONT, 19, sits in the first row with a rifle across his knees and a tin of cigarettes he is not allowed to open. PRIVATE "DOC" SUNDARAM, 30s, a medic with a faded red cross on his sleeve, picks his way down the aisle holding a crate of tinned peaches.

The distant guns thud, soft as a bass drum in another building. Nobody reacts.

DE VRIES
(to the room)
Mess in ten minutes. Tins out, men. Boots off the seats.

Beaumont lowers his boots. Pryce nods without looking up. Wróbel taps the spoon on the rim of the vat, the rhythm of a conductor calling an overture.

WRÓBEL
(to Pryce)
Thinner, Corporal. That peel is thick enough to roof a barn.

PRYCE
Sergeant, the potatoes are rotten.

WRÓBEL
Then peel the rotten out of them.

Doc Sundaram sets the crate on the stage, pries off a lid with his thumbnail, lifts a peach out, and sniffs it. He glances at the captain, shrugs, and eats a piece with his fingers while Beaumont watches with open longing and Pryce holds out a hand until Doc drops a slice into it, and the sergeant pretends not to see any of it, stirring a little harder.

SUNDARAM
(muttering)
Best thing I've eaten since Naples.

DE VRIES
Doc, you'll share that with the ward.

SUNDARAM
Always, Captain.

She makes a mark in her ledger. A flake of plaster drifts down from the ceiling and lands on the page. She blows it away.

BEAUMONT
Captain, is it true there's a piano under the stage?

DE VRIES
There is.

BEAUMONT
Does anyone play?

DE VRIES
Not since the third.

WRÓBEL
(to the soup)
I played in Kraków once. Badly.

PRYCE
You, Sergeant?

WRÓBEL
Everyone plays badly in Kraków. It is the national style.

Pryce grins into his peel. Beaumont laughs, then covers his mouth as if it were illegal. Somewhere far away a shell lands, long and low. The chandelier overhead shivers, then stills.

DE VRIES
(to the room)
Steady. That was ours.

SUNDARAM
(muttering)
Always theirs when it's close.

WRÓBEL
Tins! Line up. If you can't hold a tin, hold a spoon.

They shuffle onto the stage, one after another, and form a ragged line in the weak sun. Wróbel ladles. Steam rises from each tin into the beam of light, and for a moment the whole ruined theater looks like a painting of a feast.

PRYCE
(quietly)
Captain, what do we do when it's over?

DE VRIES
Eat.

PRYCE
And after that?

DE VRIES
After that, Corporal, we peel more potatoes.
